#--: Units

A program is built from units. A #_*"unit" is a namespace of definitions:
constants, lists, types, functions and other units. Every source file is
one unit, named by its file name without the extension -- #~"geom.z" is the unit
#~"geom" -- and a unit written inside another (#~"inner: unit { ... }") is an
#_*"inline unit", an ordinary definition of the unit around it. A unit has no
initialisation and is never imported: a name that reaches it loads it, and
only what the program reaches is checked and emitted.

A unit's name is written in lowercase ASCII letters, digits and #~"_", so that
it is the same file name on every filesystem; a file named otherwise is not
found as a unit.

#--: Finding a unit

A name the program writes is resolved outward: the unit it is written in, each
unit enclosing that one up to the file unit, then #~"core". A file unit is
found on the #_*"source roots" -- the project's #~"sourceDirs" when a
#~"project.z" names them, else the #~"--src" directories, else the current
directory -- the first root searched first, and then in the standard library
(#~"--system"). See [#> to=./zc.html: zc] (Projects) for #~"project.z" and its
settings.

A file unit may keep #_*"implementation subunits" in a directory of its own
name: #~"geom.z" beside #~"geom/" whose files are #~"geom"'s subunits. A name
written in #~"geom" looks in #~"geom/" before the roots, so a subunit can
shadow a unit of the same name elsewhere -- but only within #~"geom". A
subunit sees its parents as every unit does: it resolves outward through them.

#--: Visibility

A unit's top-level definitions are public by default. A
#~"public: unit { ... }" block restricts the unit's surface to what it lists;
an unlisted definition is private and unreachable from another unit (E0101).
An entry may rename what it exposes: #~":name" exposes #~"name" as itself,
#~"alias: internal" exposes #~"internal" as #~"alias". An implementation
subunit is private to its parent unless the parent's #~"public:" block lists
it (#~"public: unit { :impl }"), which is also what loads it for others.
Privacy governs who may reach #_*"into" a unit; it says nothing about what the
unit sees. See the Visibility section of
[#> to=./spec.html: the language specification].

#--: Generic units

A unit may be generic over types or values. An inline unit declares its
parameters in its #~"as" block (#~"mathops: unit as { t: anyval.generic ... }");
a file unit declares them at the top of the file, beside its other
definitions (#~"t: anyval.generic" in #~"cells.z"). An instance is named by
applying the unit to its arguments -- #~"(cells t: i64)", at unit level
#~"ic: (cells t: i64)" -- and is a namespace, not a value: the same arguments
name the same instance wherever they are written, and a type the unit
declares is named through it (#~"ic.Cell"). See the Generic Units section of
the specification.

#--: Toolchain requirements

A #~"require:" block states what a unit needs from the host toolchain:
#~"require: unit { library: \"quadmath\" }". It is an ordinary inline unit with
a schema layered over it: every entry must be a key the compiler consumes --
today only #~"library:", a compile-time string naming a link library -- and the
block must declare at least one (E0038). A requirement applies only when its
unit is reached: a program that never uses the unit does not link the library.
A library one operating system needs is a constant like any other: the value
folds on #~"z.build.os", which names the system the build is for (the
#~"--target" one when given), and #~"\"\"" links nothing --
#~"library: (if z.build.os == z.build.oskind.windows then { \"ws2_32\" } else { \"\" })".
See #~"z_unit.pdoc".

#--: The project unit #~"z"

Every program can read the generated unit #~"z": #~"z.build" describes the
build (the target's architecture and operating system, the triple) and
#~"z.project" the project's own #~"project.z" settings, such as
#~"z.project.sourceDirs". It is served by the compiler, with a copy on disk
under #~".zerolang/" for people and editors. See
[#> to=./z_unit.html: the z unit].

#--: Runtime-backed stdlib units

The core stdlib units (#~"system", #~"core", #~"collections",
#~"io", #~"os", #~"cli") are declaration surfaces for the C runtime:
their types and #~"is native" functions are implemented under
#~"src/runtime", while bodied free functions in them are ordinary
compiled zerolang (demand-gated). Type methods there cannot carry
#~".z" bodies. Details in the compiler doc's "Runtime-backed stdlib
units" section.

The fixtures that pin these rules: #~"hidden_subunit.z" and
#~"subunit_depth.z" (subunits, and a subunit's own subunit),
#~"public_subunit_listed.z" (a subunit a #~"public:" block lists),
#~"subunit_parent_read.z" and #~"subunit_grandparent_read.z" (a subunit
reading outward), #~"gfu_cells.z" with #~"generic_file_unit_types.z" (a
generic file unit), all under #~"tests/fixtures/emitc_corpus".
